The '''America the Beautiful quarters''' (sometimes abbreviated '''ATB quarters''') were a series of [[United States commemorative coin|circulating commemorative]] [[Quarter (United States coin)|quarters]] released by the [[United States Mint]]. Minted from 2010 through 2021, they featured unique designs commemorating a national natural or historic site such as [[List of national parks of the United States|national parks]], [[National Historic Site (United States)|national historic sites]], or [[National forest (United States)|national forests]]. There were five new reverse designs each year (one in 2021), which included one from each U.S. state, the [[Washington, D.C.|District of Columbia]], and each territory. The obverse depicts [[George Washington]] in a modified version of the portrait used for the original 1932 [[Washington quarter]].<ref>{{cite web|url=http://www.usmint.gov/mint_programs/NSQuartersProgram/ |title=About the Program |publisher=usmint.gov |access-date=July 28, 2010 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20100310194608/http://www.usmint.gov/mint_programs/NSQuartersProgram/ |archive-date=March 10, 2010 |url-status=live }}</ref>  The program was authorized by the America's Beautiful National Parks Quarter Dollar Coin Act of 2008 ({{USPL|110|456}}.<ref name=":1">{{cite web | url = http://www.gpo.gov/fdsys/pkg/PLAW-110publ456/pdf/PLAW-110publ456.pdf | publisher = usmint.gov | title = America's Beautiful National Parks Quarter Dollar Coin Act of 2008}}</ref>

==Designs and mintage==
Quarters were issued with reverse designs commemorating national parks and sites in the order of which that park or site was deemed a national site.<ref name="NSQP">{{Cite web|url=https://www.usmint.gov/learn/coin-and-medal-programs|title=U.S. Mint Coin and Medal Programs {{!}} U.S. Mint|website=www.usmint.gov|date=May 27, 2016 |access-date=March 8, 2019}}</ref> The quarters from three states depict parks or sites that were previously portrayed on the [[50 State quarters]] series ([[Grand Canyon National Park]] in Arizona, [[Yosemite National Park]] in California, and [[Mount Rushmore]] in South Dakota). While they depict the same sites, they bear new designs.<ref name="NSQP"/>

Over the course of the series, four mints produced the America the Beautiful quarters. Standard circulating quarters were produced at the [[Philadelphia Mint|Philadelphia]] and [[Denver Mint]]s throughout the series. In 2010 and 2011, the [[San Francisco Mint]] produced quarters exclusively for the annual Proof Set. In 2012, San Francisco started producing America the Beautiful quarters in the standard circulation finish of the P and D quarters for sale to collectors.<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.usmint.gov/news/press-releases/20120525-united-states-mint-to-release-the-first-america-the-beautiful-quarters-program-coin-with-the-san-francisco-mint-mark-on-june-21|title=United States Mint to Release the First America the Beautiful Quarters® Program Coin with the San Francisco Mint mark on June 21 {{!}} U.S. Mint|website=www.usmint.gov|access-date=May 10, 2019}}</ref>

On April 2, 2019, the United States Mint announced that the [[West Point Mint]] would release 10 million quarters (2 million of each design released that year).<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.usmint.gov/news/press-releases/mint-announces-w-mint-mark-circulating-quarter-collectible|title=W Mint Mark Circulating Quarter Collectible {{!}} U.S. Mint|website=www.usmint.gov|access-date=May 10, 2019}}</ref> This was the first time the mint mark appeared on a circulating coin. The quarters were mixed into uncirculated bags and rolls of the quarters to stimulate public interest in coin collecting. In 2020, the obverses of the quarters struck at West Point also include a [[privy mark]]. The privy design features the text "V75", celebrating the 75th anniversary of the end of World War II inside an outline of the [[Rainbow Pool]] at the [[World War II Memorial]] in Washington, DC.<ref>{{Cite web|title=Privy Marks on Coins {{!}} U.S. Mint|url=https://www.usmint.gov/news/inside-the-mint/privy-marks-on-coins|access-date=November 23, 2021|website=www.usmint.gov|date=April 6, 2020 }}</ref>
